#{[17592186180023 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ are false. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/bc/bc0121782b16d513d7d17d3741bd07ef1716e096bb2459fbda0cb841c7fd8b43.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $-C.$\n\n![](images/1a/1a6b109bc1da3aa7905b05f7f7902523b45fd51a65b97bec1328037bd6c0cfe7.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ only."] [17592186180026 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$∇f$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$∇f$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [17592186180027 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [17592186259803 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/eb/eb4da8143700c7d31e0ceacd09ac76a8d1bcb10379a34dce5b59d48f2ae58c13.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/70a473397216589350f63482ab3e36cb1247e7825046c70642254c8514564b00.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$ only."] [17592186259834 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the